# cww environment — copy to ~/.cww/env and fill in. Keep it mode 600.
#
# This file is loaded into every workspace container (compose env_file). It carries
# your git credential so the agent can clone and push as you. Per-user OS
# accounts keep it isolated; never commit it or share the box's account.
#
# ── Coding agent (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Which agent new workspaces run: claude (default), vibe, or opencode. Override per
# workspace with `cww create --agent <name>`, or per project by setting
# CWW_AGENT in <repo>/.cww/env. Each agent has its own Docker image
# (`cww build <agent>`).
#
# CWW_AGENT=vibe

# ── Built-in headful browser (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Every workspace runs a visible Chrome the agent drives (via chrome-devtools-
# mcp) and you can watch/take over through noVNC (port 7900 in `cww list`).
# Disable for new workspaces here (global) or in <repo>/.cww/env (per-project):
#
# CWW_BROWSER=off
#
# Virtual display size (default 1920x1080):
# CWW_BROWSER_RESOLUTION=2560x1440

# ── Claude auth (required for the claude agent) ───────────────────────────────
# The container runs Claude Code with CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN — as of this
# writing, the supported headless/container auth for a Pro/Max/Team/Enterprise
# subscription (Anthropic's auth and billing rules can change; their docs are
# the source of truth). cww does NOT copy your host Claude login into the
# container: a copied credential is unsupported across machines and can
# silently fall back to metered API usage billing. Generate a token (opens a
# browser once; uses your subscription) and paste it here:
#     claude setup-token   ->   CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=sk-ant-oat01-...
# `cww create` refuses to launch a claude workspace without it.
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=sk-ant-oat01-...

# ── Mistral auth (required for the vibe agent) ────────────────────────────────
# The container runs Mistral Vibe with MISTRAL_API_KEY (get one at
# https://console.mistral.ai). Alternatively, point Vibe at a local or other
# OpenAI-compatible endpoint by committing a .vibe/config.toml to the repo with
# a [[providers]] entry — it rides the clone into the container, and cww then
# allows creating vibe workspaces without this key.
#
# MISTRAL_API_KEY=...

# ── LLM provider key (required for the opencode agent) ───────────────────────
# OpenCode auto-detects whichever provider key is set; any ONE of these works:
#   ANTHROPIC_API_KEY / OPENAI_API_KEY / OPENROUTER_API_KEY / OPENCODE_API_KEY
# Note: a Claude Pro/Max subscription can NOT be used — OpenCode removed Claude
# OAuth login (metered API billing only).
#
# CAUTION: everything here reaches EVERY workspace container. An
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY set for opencode is also seen by claude workspaces, where
# Claude Code can prefer it over your subscription token — metered billing.
# If you use both agents, prefer one of the keyless options below, or an
# OPENROUTER_/OPENCODE_API_KEY that Claude Code ignores.
#
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
#
# Keyless alternative — a local/alternate OpenAI-compatible endpoint (e.g.
# llama.cpp's llama-server), configured in a real JSON file that cww validates
# and injects at `cww create` (opencode workspaces are then allowed without a
# key):
#   ~/.cww/opencode.json          (machine-wide)
#   <repo>/.cww/opencode.json     (per-project, wins over the global file)
# Copy examples/opencode.json.example there to start; the user guide's
# "For the opencode agent" section covers the baseURL networking pitfalls
# (LAN IP or a ~/.cww/hosts name — never the host's own hostname or
# host.docker.internal). Strict JSON, no comments.
#
# Legacy escape hatch: with no config file present, a one-line UNQUOTED
# OPENCODE_CONFIG_CONTENT={...} here still works as before (a config file
# beats this line when both exist):
#
# OPENCODE_CONFIG_CONTENT={"provider":{...},"model":"..."}

# ── Git credential ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The container authenticates over HTTPS as  <CWW_GIT_USER>:<CWW_GIT_TOKEN>.
#
# CWW_GIT_USER  = your platform login (the token owner's username).
#   - Forgejo / Gitea: REQUIRED — auth fails without your real login.
#   - GitHub: leave unset to use fine-grained / App tokens (x-access-token),
#     or set your login to use a classic PAT.
#
# CWW_GIT_TOKEN = a personal access token scoped as tightly as possible —
#   ideally pinned to the one repo, with repository contents read/write and
#   nothing else. What that means per platform (UIs move; see your platform's
#   docs for the current path):
#   - GitHub: a fine-grained token limited to the repo, Contents: Read and write.
#   - Forgejo / Gitea: an access token with repository Read and Write; newer
#     Forgejo can scope it to specific repositories.
#   Single-repo isolation when the token UI can't scope to one repo (older
#     Gitea, or Forgejo without the per-repo option): use a dedicated machine
#     account that is only added as a collaborator on that one repo.

CWW_GIT_USER=your-login
CWW_GIT_TOKEN=paste-token-here

# ── Clone URL override (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# By default cww derives the in-container clone URL from your local 'origin'
# remote, rewriting SSH remotes to https on the default port 443. Set this when
# that guess is wrong — e.g. a self-hosted Forgejo/Gitea served over plain http
# or on a non-443 port, or a web host that differs from the SSH host. Used
# verbatim (auth still comes from CWW_GIT_USER/CWW_GIT_TOKEN via the helper).
# NEVER embed credentials in the URL (https://user:token@host/...): a
# <repo>/.cww/env holding this value may end up committed, and the token
# belongs only in this global, never-committed ~/.cww/env.
#
# IMPORTANT: set CWW_REPO_URL in the PER-PROJECT <repo>/.cww/env, NOT here.
# `cww create` sources this global file first, then the project file, so a
# per-project value applies to that repo only. A CWW_REPO_URL in THIS global
# file pins EVERY project to the same clone URL — the footgun where `cww create`
# inside repo B silently clones repo A. Leave it commented here; put it there:
#
#   # <repo>/.cww/env   (gitignore this file)
#   CWW_REPO_URL=http://forgejo.example:3000/owner/repo.git

# ── Container hostname resolution (not set here) ──────────────────────────────
# The container resolves names via the Docker daemon's DNS, NOT your host's
# /etc/hosts or mDNS. If your git host (or any service) only resolves on the
# host — e.g. an internal name or an mDNS ".local" — the container's clone will
# fail with a DNS error. Map it in a separate hosts file (one "hostname ip" per
# line), which cww renders into the container's /etc/hosts:
#   ~/.cww/hosts              (global, all workspaces)
#   <repo>/.cww/hosts         (per-project)
# Example line:  buildbox 192.168.1.10
# To reach services on the Docker host itself (e.g. a local llama-server), use
# the host's real hostname or LAN IP — usually no mapping needed at all, since
# container DNS forwards to host DNS. (host.docker.internal:host-gateway does
# NOT work under rootless Docker: published ports are forwarded on the host's
# real interfaces only, so nothing listens on the gateway IP.)

# ── Anything else ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Add any other env your app's services need; everything here is visible to the
# container.
